    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.surround\n\n### Description\n\nWhen deleting or replacing bracket-like surroundings in multiline sections, whitespace, in particular new lines, are removed in an undesirable way that, depending on the language, may make the resulting code ungrammatical. However, tags are not similarly affected.\n\n### Neovim version\n\nNVIM v0.10.1 Build type: Release LuaJIT 2.1.1713484068\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nIf the following is `repro.lua`\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nvim.env.LAZY_STDPATH = \".repro\"\r\nload(vim.fn.system(\"curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/folke/lazy.nvim/main/bootstrap.lua\"))()\r\n\r\nrequire(\"lazy.minit\").repro({\r\n\tspec = {\r\n\t\t{ \"echasnovski/mini.surround\", version = false, opts = {} },\r\n\t},\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nand this is `example`\r\n\r\n```\r\nif (condition) { \r\n\tlet stuff = ( // comment\r\n\t\tfoo,\r\n\t\tbar,\r\n\t);\r\n}\r\n\r\n<t1>\r\n\t<t2><t2/>\r\n</t1>\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. Go to line 2, `sd{`. The relevant part of `example` becomes:\r\n```\r\nif (condition) let stuff = ( // comment\r\n\t\tfoo,\r\n\t\tbar,\r\n\t);\r\n```\r\n2. Undo, then go to line 3, `sd(`. `example` becomes\r\n```\r\n\tlet stuff = // comment\r\n\t\tfoo,\r\n\t\tbar,;\r\n```\r\n3. Notice how the trailing comment kept the upper new line from being removed.\r\n4. Undo and then try `sr({` on line 3:\r\n```\r\n\tlet stuff = { // comment\r\n\t\tfoo,\r\n\t\tbar, };\r\n```\r\n5. Finally, go to line 9, `sdt`\r\n```\r\n\r\n\t<t2><t2/>\r\n\r\n```\r\n6. Notice how the surrounding tags are simply removed without affecting any other formatting.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe behavior should be consistent across different surroundings. In particular, it should look most like the tag behavior, i.e. the situation where formatting is minimally affected. It should not be sucking up newlines.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nSee Steps to reproduce.",
