        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Yes, this is indeed the case of [difference between opening and closing parenthesis/brackets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clapperboard). And as newline character is whitespace, it is also included in the `{` / `[` / `(` surroundings. Tip for future: you can (temporarily) highlight the (found with `sd`, `sr`, `sf`, `sF`) surrounding with `sh`. For example, `sh{` or `sh(`.\r\n\r\n> Most of what I reported is intended behavior, as it turns out. I hadn't realized that in/out brackets behave differently as _input_ these modes (I'm pretty sure that only mattered for _output_ in `vim-surround`, which is my reference point).\r\n\r\nYou can address this at least these approaches:\r\n- Use closing character instead of opening: `sd)`, `sd}`.\r\n- Modify input part of relevant surroundings to include only single character on left and right. With something like this:\r\n    ```lua\r\n    require('mini.surround').setup({\r\n      custom_surroundings = {\r\n        ['('] = { input = { '%b()', '^.().*().$' } },\r\n        ['['] = { input = { '%b[]', '^.().*().$' } },\r\n        ['{'] = { input = { '%b{}', '^.().*().$' } },\r\n      },\r\n    })\r\n    ```",
